WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.070
- Everybody, we are back,

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:03.230
and in the second season of
"The Kelly Clarkson Show"

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:04.900
we introduced something new,

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.550
something to get us out of the studio

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:09.030
and face to face with guests safely.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.770
I'd hop in the car and head to the movies

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.333
for what we call our drive interviews.

00:00:14.715 --> 00:00:17.548
♪ Drive-Interview ♪

00:00:19.292 --> 00:00:20.629
- My favorite one.

00:00:20.629 --> 00:00:21.462
♪ Ooh ♪

00:00:21.462 --> 00:00:22.490
That's my favorite one.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:24.510
I got to catch up with so many people.

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.040
Jay Leno, Cedric the
Entertainer, Neve Campbell,

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:28.610
Dr. Phil, just to name a few.

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:29.670
Check out some of the moments

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.764
we've never shared with y'all before.

00:00:31.764 --> 00:00:34.347
(upbeat music)

00:00:40.795 --> 00:00:41.942
- What's up?

00:00:41.942 --> 00:00:42.775
- Hey.

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:44.430
Fancy meeting you here.
- How are you?

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:46.940
I am so excited that
you're here at the drive-in

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.772
and I am too cause I'm a huge fan.

00:00:48.772 --> 00:00:50.105
- Aww.

00:00:50.105 --> 00:00:52.310
- You look very at home in this truck.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:53.360
- You look very at home.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:54.670
What kind of wheels are those?

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:55.503
What do you got?

00:00:55.503 --> 00:00:56.893
- This is a '57 Chevy.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:02.147
- This is a 1970 Dodge Challenger.

00:01:02.147 --> 00:01:04.160
- I hear you have an eight year old son

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:05.440
that's actually teaching you about cars,

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:06.680
and I don't know if you
need to be taught though,

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.580
cause this is a pretty
cool car you're riding.

00:01:11.565 --> 00:01:13.560
What was the first car you ever drove?

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:14.960
Cause that's a pretty fly car.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.000
- The first car I ever drove
was a '77 Coupe DeVille.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.539
It was my mom's car.

00:01:20.539 --> 00:01:23.020
And I did the thing where I got a wreck,

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.150
where I'd, you know...

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:24.983
Well, it wasn't a bad one.

00:01:24.983 --> 00:01:27.610
I just scraped it on the passenger side

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.980
and she never saw it
because she would drive.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:30.985
- And you didn't tell her?

00:01:30.985 --> 00:01:31.818
- What?

00:01:31.818 --> 00:01:32.651
No.

00:01:32.651 --> 00:01:33.484
- What?

00:01:33.484 --> 00:01:34.317
- What?

00:01:34.317 --> 00:01:37.830
So then when she finally
discovered it, I was like, really?

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.740
I wonder when that happened.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:40.573
- Who did that?

00:01:40.573 --> 00:01:41.453
- That's terrible.

00:01:43.431 --> 00:01:45.950
- Do you think driving,
I mean, I don't know,

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:47.120
do you think it is therapeutic?

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.710
I feel it's therapeutic.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:51.110
- It can be, if you're
not in this LA traffic,

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:51.943
which hasn't been bad.

00:01:51.943 --> 00:01:53.660
That's one good thing
of the pandemic, right?

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:55.526
The traffic hasn't been so bad.

00:01:55.526 --> 00:01:56.359
- Yeah.

00:01:56.359 --> 00:01:57.192
- But it can be.

00:01:57.192 --> 00:01:59.960
I mean, particularly if
you kind of kick back,

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.460
put on some tunes, put your
cruise control on and roll.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:05.703
It really can be kind of therapeutic.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.080
- I like to get out and, you know, drive,

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:11.970
especially like during the pandemic,

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:12.803
that's what I've been doing.

00:02:12.803 --> 00:02:14.420
Like just kind of riding
around, hanging out.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:15.650
- I feel like I'm in high school again.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:17.650
That's what I used to do
when I first got a car.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:18.483
- Oh yeah, yeah.

00:02:18.483 --> 00:02:19.970
- I was like, I'm gonna drive around.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:20.840
Do you sing in the car?

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.420
Cause I am the tool that
you will pull up next to

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:24.710
and I am jamming.

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.994
- Yeah, well Kelly, you can jam.

00:02:27.994 --> 00:02:29.500
- I look like an idiot though.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:30.879
It's not like cool jam.

00:02:30.879 --> 00:02:33.458
- I sound like a pig under a gate.

00:02:33.458 --> 00:02:35.543
- Oh my God.
(laughing)

00:02:35.543 --> 00:02:38.859
- I gotta totally be by myself
though with the windows up.

00:02:38.859 --> 00:02:40.650
- You won't let people hear.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:41.483
- Oh hell no.

00:02:41.483 --> 00:02:43.970
- You're my favorite people
at karaoke bars, by the way.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:45.290
The ones that aren't good.

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:47.620
- Yeah, no I'm your favorite
people at karaoke bars,

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:49.799
just sit in the back and
watch people that sing.

00:02:49.799 --> 00:02:52.660
- Well, until we get just enough alcohol,

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:54.130
like a little liquid courage in you,

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:56.142
and then you're like all
right, I can do this.

00:02:56.142 --> 00:02:58.420
- Yeah, you ain't got a big
enough jug to get me up there.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:02:59.770
- You should try moonshine.

00:03:01.182 --> 00:03:02.870
Do you have a favorite movie

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:04.310
that you've ever seen at a drive-in?

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:05.143
- Yeah.

00:03:05.143 --> 00:03:06.940
I can just go back to
like the first "Shaft",

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:10.160
like being a kid, like seeing
that at a drive-in movie,

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.480
and then Bruce Lee movies.

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:14.040
Those were the ones that
we used to go and see,

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.837
like the cool Kung Fu
movies when they came out.

00:03:16.837 --> 00:03:18.014
"Fist of Fury".

00:03:18.014 --> 00:03:19.270
- The fury, yeah.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:20.103
- "Fist of Fury".

00:03:20.103 --> 00:03:22.800
- My first one was actually a Batman,

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:23.790
the first Batman.
- Oh really?

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.440
With the best Batman, Michael Keaton.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:26.630
- Michael Keaton as Batman, of course.

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:28.227
That's the best Batman.

00:03:28.227 --> 00:03:29.400
- Yeah I love 'em all,
but no, Michael Keaton,

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.270
he wins it for me.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:31.380
- Yeah of course, he's so cool.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.640
- It's cool, but I cam for
the snacks, I'm gonna be real.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.340
I like the snacks.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:36.173
- I have snacks.

00:03:36.173 --> 00:03:38.063
I've got some Good &amp; Plentys,
I got some Junior Mints.

00:03:38.063 --> 00:03:40.540
- Junior Mints, that's my mom's favorite.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:42.000
- That's my kids' favorite.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.550
- I love Milk Duds, but whatever,

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:44.650
but they stick in your teeth.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:45.580
I don't like that part.

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:46.520
- Wait, I have a question though,

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.410
because I have a theory about
Red Viness versus Twizzlers.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:50.340
- Twizzlers all the way.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.040
- Okay, so everyone from the east coast,

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:53.320
but you're not east
coast, likes Twizzlers,

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:55.287
and everyone out here in
California likes Red Vines,

00:03:55.287 --> 00:03:57.580
and I can't eat Red Vines.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:58.700
- Since I've been in California,

00:03:58.700 --> 00:03:59.810
it's all about the Red Vine game.

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:02.090
- Okay, that's a little bit
of an imitation game for me.

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:04.220
- My wife is a Red Viner, so you know,

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:05.900
that made me a Red Viner at that point.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:06.860
- Okay, I like it.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:07.980
- Once your wife becomes something...

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.463
That's it, that's it.
- Bit of compromise.

00:04:09.463 --> 00:04:10.296
- Twizzlers are way better.

00:04:10.296 --> 00:04:12.490
- And I chew off the ends
and I get a Coke and then...

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:13.340
Yep, yep.

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:14.190
That's what I do.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:19.340
What have you found yourself
doing like during quarantine,

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:20.370
like since we've seen each other?

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:23.454
- Well, you see, I look
at the positive side

00:04:23.454 --> 00:04:25.510
of this corona thing.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:27.110
- Can you tell me what that is?

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.740
- Well, that is, as a guy, go, honey,

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:32.090
I'd love to take you to
that new vegan restaurant,

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:34.800
but it's closed, we can't...

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.760
Honey, I'd love to see "Hamilton",

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.010
but the theaters, they're
not doing it right now.

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:41.550
Honey, I want, I'd love
to take you to Europe,

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:43.090
but they won't let us in.

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:45.893
So for a lot of guys, this
is actually pretty good.

00:04:45.893 --> 00:04:48.200
- It's like seems like
we've just gotta stay here.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:49.033
- Yeah.

00:04:49.033 --> 00:04:51.750
Honey, I'll go to In N
Out, I'll get some burgers,

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:54.144
and bring 'em back and
we'll have a dinner here.

00:04:54.144 --> 00:04:55.550
- That is an upside, I guess.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:56.450
- Yeah, exactly.

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:57.733
- It's amazing.

00:04:57.733 --> 00:04:58.854
You're an optimist.

00:04:58.854 --> 00:04:59.727
- I'm an optimist.

00:04:59.727 --> 00:05:02.093
That's right.
- You are.

00:05:02.093 --> 00:05:05.060
- Well, hey, we've gotta do this again.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:05.893
- We do.

00:05:05.893 --> 00:05:06.842
I love meeting up like this.

00:05:06.842 --> 00:05:07.675
You know what it's like?

00:05:07.675 --> 00:05:10.280
It's like an old detective
movie where we got like,

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:12.567
I got some stuff, maybe
I dumped some stuff

00:05:12.567 --> 00:05:14.470
off in the trunk and then you say hey-

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:16.000
- Maybe I've got a body
in the truck, who knows?

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:16.833
- You have.

00:05:16.833 --> 00:05:18.623
You've got the perfect trunk for a body.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:21.980
- Did you grow up doing drive-ins?

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:22.813
- Oh yeah.

00:05:22.813 --> 00:05:24.130
- I think my mother has told me the story

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:28.180
that my father made my mother
get in the trunk of a car

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:30.918
so he didn't have to pay
or something to get her in.

00:05:30.918 --> 00:05:33.233
- Oh yeah, that was a thing.

00:05:39.718 --> 00:05:41.820
- [Kelly] That's truly a muscle car.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:43.140
- [Wilmer] His name is Spawn.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:45.720
- His name is Spawn, and
where did that come from?

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.320
- So, one of my favorite
action comic books

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:50.790
is by the name of "Spawn", is basically,

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:52.610
it's this comic book character that

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:54.510
became really big and
underground in the nineties.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:57.343
And then eventually I started
naming things after him.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:05:59.280
- His name is Sunny.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.450
I saw him literally driving by in Texas,

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:03.300
like far away in a dealership.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:04.900
And I was like, he will be mine.

00:06:05.907 --> 00:06:07.400
- [Wilmer] Was it yellow?

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:08.233
- Yeah.

00:06:08.233 --> 00:06:09.360
I love him.
- That's amazing.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:10.716
- [Wilmer] That's incredible.

00:06:10.716 --> 00:06:11.549
- He looked like he was like straight up

00:06:11.549 --> 00:06:13.270
out of like "Fried Green
Tomatoes", the movie,

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:14.430
and I was like, you're cute.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.740
It's wood paneling
floorboard, it's adorable.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:17.573
- Oh, man.

00:06:17.573 --> 00:06:18.410
That's so good.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:20.063
- Everything is still old school on it.

00:06:20.063 --> 00:06:21.271
- It's all original?

00:06:21.271 --> 00:06:22.160
- Mm-hmm, yeah, I love it.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:24.120
- [Wilmer] That's a tough
one to keep, you know,

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:25.200
tuned up if you're not driving it.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:26.910
You've gotta spin that one a little.

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:28.860
- Yeah, like I can change
oil and change a tire,

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.190
and that's really what I can do.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:32.210
- First of all, your change oil?

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:34.490
I haven't changed oil
in probably 20 years.

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:36.220
- Oh I haven't changed oil in a while,

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:38.260
but I can change oil.

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:39.093
- I'm still impressed.

00:06:39.093 --> 00:06:40.730
- I think Texas women, I think
they make you in the south,

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:41.860
at least learn how to do it

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:42.950
if you're on the side of the road.

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:44.880
- See you for you and the south,

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:46.060
they teach you how to change oil.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:49.310
For us, they teach us how to
salsa dance at three years old.

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:50.213
- We line dance.

00:06:51.234 --> 00:06:52.870
I would have preferred...

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:54.400
I like line dancing
but salsa would've been

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:55.430
a lot more sexier.

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:57.150
But, anyway.

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.190
Are you the kind of guy that
has family like all around you?

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:00.023
Do they live near you?

00:07:00.023 --> 00:07:00.856
- Yeah, yeah.

00:07:00.856 --> 00:07:03.190
You know, a little while ago
I bought the neighbor's house

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:04.700
and I moved my mom there.

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:06.330
The timing couldn't have been better

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:08.762
because then pandemic hits.

00:07:08.762 --> 00:07:10.250
- Does that feel so good to be able to

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:11.740
give that back to your mom like that?

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:13.890
Like she gave so much
of herself for you to...

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:14.723
- No, it's amazing.

00:07:14.723 --> 00:07:15.556
It's unbelievable.

00:07:15.556 --> 00:07:17.330
And honestly, when we
came to the United States,

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:18.530
we were all we had.

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:19.840
We had to start from scratch.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:23.200
My mom was a big factor
of why we turned out

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:24.920
any kind of reasonable, you know,

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:27.540
and she also gave something back to me

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:31.121
when I ate Colombian
food the entire pandemic.

00:07:31.121 --> 00:07:33.520
- Nostalgia in isolating time.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:34.410
- Oh, for sure.

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:35.243
For sure.

00:07:35.243 --> 00:07:37.940
But I think that like, you
know, the food was incredible,

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:39.710
and to just to sit with
my mom and not having...

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:41.857
You know how it is, we're traveling,

00:07:41.857 --> 00:07:44.010
if we're not on set, we're doing...

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:46.027
You know, we're showing
up for social causes.

00:07:46.027 --> 00:07:47.180
- It's the first time we had to slow down.

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:49.910
- Yeah, first time to
really look at each other

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:51.790
and really talk to one another,

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:53.680
besides like the short talk, right?

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:54.650
Like, how are you?

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:57.335
Good, all right, great,
who did that, who did this?

00:07:57.335 --> 00:07:59.800
But this was the first
time where like, you know,

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:02.363
my fiance got to ask
questions like, you know,

00:08:02.363 --> 00:08:04.250
what were you like, like a kid, you know?

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:06.010
And I found out so many
things about my mom

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:07.840
that I was just like, I don't know why

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:08.850
I never asked you that.

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:09.683
You know.

00:08:09.683 --> 00:08:11.060
And just when you think
you know your parents,

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:12.260
you really don't.

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:15.020
And we don't ever ask our
parents or grandparents questions

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:16.760
about their childhood
or how they were like,

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:18.290
or whatever, we take it for what it is.

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:20.040
- And you start to see your parent

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:22.970
as like a 20 something
year old in their stories

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:24.210
and you're like, man,
I would have probably

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:25.478
been friends with you.

00:08:25.478 --> 00:08:26.721
Like that's so cool.

00:08:26.721 --> 00:08:27.770
- Like my mom was telling
me about ex-boyfriends

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:29.550
and so I was like, what,
you had ex-boyfriends?

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:31.072
- Yeah, we hate him.

00:08:31.072 --> 00:08:33.047
- Oh we hate him.

00:08:33.047 --> 00:08:36.840
Yeah, so that was definitely
a big moment for me too,

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:39.030
to kind of get to know her even more.

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:40.350
- You're so great.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:41.510
- You are so great.

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:42.515
Are you kidding?

00:08:42.515 --> 00:08:43.930
- I mean you're already
like a really cool guy

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:45.470
in the limelight, I just mean like, wow,

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:46.660
you're like such a family guy.

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:47.493
That's so cool.

00:08:47.493 --> 00:08:48.326
- [Wilmer] Oh, thank you.

00:08:48.326 --> 00:08:50.190
- Not everybody is like
open and vulnerable

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:52.170
and like, you know, some
people can be guarded.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:54.210
- I think all of us male actors, you know,

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:56.810
we're lead to believe that we have to say,

00:08:56.810 --> 00:08:59.880
sound, look a certain way.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:01.656
I'm too Latin for that, man.

00:09:01.656 --> 00:09:05.124
You know, I'm from Colombia
and Venezuela, like,

00:09:05.124 --> 00:09:07.470
what you see is what you get with me.

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:11.480
- Well, before we go, can I
hear like a rev from Spawn?

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:12.323
- Sure, sure.

00:09:14.822 --> 00:09:16.744
(engine revs)

00:09:16.744 --> 00:09:17.577
- Woo!

00:09:19.028 --> 00:09:20.361
I love your car!

00:09:24.196 --> 00:09:26.464
All right, I'll see you next time!

00:09:26.464 --> 00:09:28.131
- See you next time!

